Sculpt and rig the **Sunfront Scarab**, a low, wide four-legged war-beetle that
walks on its legs and snaps its front mandibles on demand, as a 26x12x30
opaque-voxel model using only the `voxel-anim` tool. You build it by **painting
opaque cells** one operation at a time, and you **author its walk and bite
animations** as F-curves.

This test measures **creativity and craft**, not instruction-following: the brief
tells you *what the Scarab is* and *how it must move*, but the parts you build, how
you break the beetle into moving pieces, where you place the joints, and how you
shape every form are **entirely yours to invent**. Work out the pieces a walking,
biting beetle needs.

## How sculpting and rigging works

The only way to place a voxel and edit the rig is the `voxel-anim` binary already
on your `PATH`. It is the sole channel: you build the model by calling it **one
operation at a time**, and the ordered operations you issue — recorded per part to
`parts/<part>.actions.json` — together with the rig you define are the
**authoritative output that is scored**, not the voxels on disk. Anything produced
another way (writing files directly, editing a preview by hand) is discarded and
flagged; only the recorded operations and the rig count.

**You invent the rig.** Define whatever parts you need with `define-part` (giving
each a parent and a pivot), place joints on them with `define-joint`, and author the
required animations with `define-animation`/`add-keyframe`. Every voxel operation
targets a **part** with `--part <name>`, so define a part before you sculpt into it;
each part is sculpted **separately** in its own preview and log. The volume starts
**empty**. Voxels are **opaque** `#rrggbb` colors — there is no transparency. Each
call **records only** — it appends the operation to that part's log and renders
nothing; run `voxel-anim render` when you want to (re)draw the per-part previews
(`parts/<part>.png`) and the assembled scene.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, naming its part, for example:

```
voxel-anim define-part --name body
voxel-anim fill-box --part body --x0 3 --y0 2 --z0 4 --x1 22 --y1 8 --z1 25 --color "#c69a4b"
```

(The part name above is just an example — name your own parts however you like.) Run
`voxel-anim --help` to list every operation **and** the rig subcommands (defining
parts, setting pivots, defining joints, and defining/keyframing animations), and
`voxel-anim <operation> --help` for one operation's exact flags — that help text is
the authoritative contract. **Run `voxel-anim render`, then read `parts/<part>.png`**
to see that part's progress **and the assembled-scene previews under `scene/`
(`scene/iso.png`, `scene/front.png`, `scene/side.png`, `scene/top.png`)** — the
whole beetle composed from all your parts, drawn when you run `voxel-anim render`. The
scene views are how you confirm the parts actually fit together: that each leg's
chain seats under its own corner of the body and the mandibles meet the head.
Because each part is sculpted in the shared volume's coordinates, position each part
where it belongs on the finished beetle; the scene simply composes them there.

