## What to deliver

Build and rig the beetle up operation by operation until each `parts/<part>.png`
matches the brief, the assembled `scene/` previews show the parts fitting together
correctly, and the rig is complete: a domed carapace body (the fixed root) on four
independent, articulated legs (each a chain with a bent-knee standing rest), with a
pair of snapping mandibles at the head. **Author the two required animations** as
F-curves with the `voxel-anim` animation subcommands: a **`walk`** (a diagonal-pair
gait whose feet plant flat and still, then lift and swing — with weight, not a
weightless slide) and a **`bite`** (the mandibles snapping open and shut while the
legs stay planted). The animations are the fixed contract — produce both, by those
names; the parts and joints that realize them are yours. You may add your own extra
parts, joints, or animations on top, but do not drop or contradict the required
`walk` and `bite`. Before you finish, run `voxel-anim render` so it emits the
per-part `.glb` geometry your result is built from — an unrendered part is scored as
empty (`voxel-anim render --component <part>` redraws one part; `voxel-anim render
--time <ms> --animation <name>` renders the model posed at that instant of an
animation to check the motion). When you are satisfied, stop: the recorded per-part
logs and `rig.json` are your finished submission.
